---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Stage from "../components/Stage.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import { readFileSync, existsSync } from "node:fs";
import { resolve, dirname } from "node:path";
// The version on this page is the version on disk: an install line typed by hand goes stale.
let dir = process.cwd();
while (!existsSync(resolve(dir, "packages/ivolt/package.json")) && dirname(dir) !== dir) dir = dirname(dir);
const version = JSON.parse(readFileSync(resolve(dir, "packages/ivolt/package.json"), "utf8")).version;
const tag = /-rc/.test(version) ? "next" : /beta/.test(version) ? "beta" : "latest";
---
<Base title="Getting started" description="Use iVOLT from local files without a build step, or as ES modules with real package exports. Browser support and coexistence with existing CSS.">
  <article class="docs-prose">
    <h1>Getting started</h1>
    <p>iVOLT ships as prebuilt CSS and JavaScript. Two files are enough: a stylesheet and a module. There is no build step, no configuration file and no runtime dependency.</p>
    <p class="docs-meta"><strong>Version:</strong> <code>{version}</code>. <strong>Licence:</strong> MIT. <strong>Engines tested:</strong> Chromium, Firefox, WebKit.</p>

    <Stage primary name="button/variants" title="The framework, running on its own page" />

    <Section title="Install from npm" lead="The release candidate is published under the next tag; the contract is frozen for 1.0 and only fixes land before it.">
      <Code label="shell" panels={[{ key: "src", label: "SHELL", code: `npm install @intervolutions/ivolt@${tag}` }]} />
      <p>Pin the exact version in production. What is public, and how it is allowed to change, is written down in <a href="/foundations/stability">stability</a>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Build the package" lead="From a checkout, the same files the registry serves.">
      <Code label="shell" panels={[{ key: "src", label: "SHELL", code: `git clone https://github.com/iNTERVOLUTIONS-Labs/iVOLT
cd iVOLT
npm ci
npm run build          # writes packages/ivolt/dist` }]} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Plain HTML, no Node" lead="Copy packages/ivolt/dist next to your page and link the files.">
      <p>Add the <code>iv-root</code> class where base typography should apply; on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> for a whole page, or on a wrapper when embedding into an existing site.</p>
      <Code label="index.html" panels={[{ key: "src", label: "INDEX.HTML", code: `<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" class="iv-root" data-iv-theme="system">
<head>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="ivolt/css/ivolt.min.css">
  <script type="module" src="ivolt/js/auto.js"></script>
</head>` }]} />
      <p>Prefer a classic script? <code>ivolt/js/ivolt.iife.min.js</code> exposes one global, <code>IVOLT</code>, and does not initialise anything until you call <code>IVOLT.init()</code>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="ES modules with a bundler" lead="The package exports are the public API; deep paths into dist/ are not.">
      <Code label="javascript" panels={[{ key: "src", label: "JAVASCRIPT", code: `import "@intervolutions/ivolt/css/ivolt.css";
import { init, Dialog } from "@intervolutions/ivolt";
import { setTheme } from "@intervolutions/ivolt/theme";

init(document);                       // explicit, idempotent
const dialog = Dialog.get(document.querySelector("#signup"));` }]} />
      <p>Importing <code>@intervolutions/ivolt</code> has no side effects; only <code>@intervolutions/ivolt/auto</code> initialises on load. Modules never touch <code>document</code> at import time, so they are safe in server-side rendering.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Pick the CSS you need">
      <div class="docs-scroller">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Contains</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>css/ivolt.css</code></td><td>Everything, organised in cascade layers <code>iv.reset, iv.tokens, iv.base, iv.layout, iv.components, iv.utilities, iv.overrides</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>css/ivolt.flat.css</code></td><td>Same rules without <code>@layer</code>, for sites whose existing CSS is not layered and must keep competing by specificity.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>css/core.css</code></td><td>Tokens, base and layout only.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>css/components/*.css</code></td><td>One file per component, unlayered. Import after <code>core.css</code>; assign a layer yourself if you use them: <code>@import url("…/components/button.css") layer(iv.components);</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>css/reset.css</code></td><td>Optional normalisation. It is unlayered: import it with <code>@import url("…/reset.css") layer(iv.reset)</code>, or link <code>css/reset.layer.css</code>, which is already wrapped.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Both sheets are on the stage above: the <b>flat sheet</b> control swaps the layered stylesheet for the unlayered one on a page that is already running.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Coexisting with existing CSS">
      <p>Cascade layers order iVOLT's own rules; they do not isolate it. Any normal (non-<code>!important</code>) rule outside a layer beats every layered rule, whatever its specificity. A theme rule like <code>button &#123; background: red &#125;</code> therefore overrides <code>.iv-button</code> in <code>ivolt.css</code>. Two ways out: use <code>ivolt.flat.css</code>, or wrap the legacy stylesheet in a layer declared before iVOLT's: <code>@import url("theme.css") layer(legacy);</code>. Base element styles only apply inside <code>.iv-root</code>, so headings, links and inputs outside it are left alone.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Browser support">
      <p>Designed for Chrome and Edge 111+, Firefox 113+, Safari and iOS 16.4+, Samsung Internet 22+. The build targets these versions; container queries and <code>popover</code> are progressive enhancements. No Internet Explorer. Automated tests run in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit through Playwright, which is not the same as testing every physical device.</p>
    </Section>
  </article>
</Base>
